<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Go to the Window. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Go to the Window.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Respond to classroom commands.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify colors by naming and pointing in response to classroom commands (e.g., 'Point to the red chair.' 'What color is this?').
Can respond to classroom commands with gestures or one-word answers (e.g., 'Show me the yellow pencil, please.' and the students hold up a yellow pencil).
Can answer simple questions about location, using prepositions (e.g., 'Is the book on the table?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>Students are engaged and prepared for the lesson. Prior knowledge is activated, and new language is introduced through clear modeling and concept checking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using classroom objects and locations introduced in previous lessons. Examples: "Simon Says, point to the red book!" "Simon Says, touch the blue chair!" "Simon Says, go to the door!" (incorporating the theme 'Go to the Window' indirectly). This activates vocabulary like 'red, blue, book, chair, desk' and verbs 'point, touch, go'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a classroom object (e.g., a book) and places it 'on' a table, then 'under' a chair, and 'next to' a box. Each time, the teacher clearly says, "The book is ON the table." "The book is UNDER the chair." "The book is NEXT TO the box." The teacher models the /w/ sound in "Where" by exaggerating it and the /b/ sound in "book." The teacher asks, "Where is the book?" and answers, "It's on the table." (Teacher points to the location). The teacher also models polite requests: "Show me the yellow pencil, please." while holding up a yellow pencil.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher places a pencil under a desk) "Is the pencil on the desk?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher places a book in a bag) "Where is the book? Is it in the bag or under the bag?" (Expected answer: "In the bag!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a yellow marker) "What color is this?" (Expected answer: "Yellow!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>New language content is presented in a meaningful context related to the scenario and theme. Students observe and process the input.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?":</b> The teacher uses large, colorful flashcards or a simple illustrated chart depicting a classroom scene with various objects (chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil) placed in different locations using prepositions (in, on, under, next to, behind, in front of, between, near). The teacher points to each item and its location, saying, "Look! The pencil is IN the box." "The book is ON the table." "The bag is UNDER the chair." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /w/ in 'where' and /b/ in 'book' when asking and answering questions about the image. Letter sounds K, L, M, N, O are reinforced through flashcards or an alphabet chart in the classroom.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated classroom scene chart/poster, flashcards of classroom objects (chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil, marker, ruler, glue, folder), flashcards of prepositions, realia (actual classroom items). Genre: Illustrated vocabulary presentation, short descriptive phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions (e.g., 'The book is on the desk.'). Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions (e.g., 'Go to the window.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What do you see?" (Expected: "Book!", "Desk!", "Chair!") "Where are we?" (Expected: "Classroom!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pointing:</b> Teacher asks, "Point to the book on the table!" "Point to the pencil in the box!" Students point to the correct items on the chart/flashcards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>One-word answers:</b> Teacher points to an object in the chart, "Where is the ruler?" (Expected: "Under!") "Where is the marker?" (Expected: "On!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Students practice new language in controlled and semi-controlled activities, focusing on accuracy in Speaking. They solidify grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: Responding to "Where is...?" questions and classroom commands using prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to, behind, in front of, between, near) and nouns (chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil, poster, clip, ruler, glue, marker, folder, card, shelf, chart, pen, chalk, key, notebook) and colors (yellow, red, blue, orange, green, purple). Using polite requests ("Show me the blue crayon, please.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Action Commands (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher gives direct commands using TPR. "Go to the window!" (Students move). "Point to the blue chair!" (Students point). "Pick up the red book!" (Students pick it up). "Put the pencil on the desk!" (Students follow). The teacher ensures students respond with actions and, when appropriate, simple one-word confirmations like "Okay!" or "Done!". This reinforces 'go, point, pick, put' and various colors and nouns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Where is...?" Game with Realia (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher hides a classroom object (e.g., a yellow marker, a green book, a small box) in a visible location. The teacher asks, "Where is the yellow marker?" Students respond chorally or individually with one-word answers, "On!" "Under!" "In!". Then the teacher shows the location, "Yes! It's ON the desk." The teacher repeats with different objects and prepositions, encouraging students to use a wider range of prepositions and colors. The teacher models the /w/ sound in 'where' and the /b/ sound in 'book' for each question.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Show Me" Pair Activity (Peer Check/Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a small collection of classroom objects (e.g., a pencil, a ruler, a small book, a colorful card). Student A politely asks Student B, "Show me the blue card, please." Student B identifies the object and holds it up, saying, "Here it is!" or simply "Blue card." They switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially for polite requests and object names. Students also practice identifying plural nouns minimally if they have "two pens," for example.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Students use the target language spontaneously and fluently in a realistic communicative task, applying grammar and vocabulary to complete the scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?":</b> Students participate in a "Classroom Treasure Hunt" game. The teacher hides a few "treasures" (small toys or colorful classroom items like a red marker, a green clip, a small blue box) around the classroom. Students are divided into small groups or work individually. The teacher gives a simple command for the first item, e.g., "Go to the red chair. Look UNDER the chair!" Students 'go to' the location, 'look under' the object, and find the item. When they find it, they shout out its location or name, e.g., "Under!" "Red marker!" For the next item, the teacher asks, "Where is the green clip?" and students have to use their knowledge to guess the location by pointing or suggesting a preposition, e.g., "Next to the door!" "In the box!" The teacher confirms or gives another hint. The game continues with different locations and commands, like "Is the book on the table?" or "Point to the poster behind the shelf."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme: "Go to the Window." and Scenario: "Where Is It?":</b> The activity naturally connects to the theme by requiring students to move around the classroom, including moving towards the window area if a "treasure" is hidden near it ("Go to the window! Look NEAR the window!"). It directly addresses the scenario "Where Is It?" by prompting students to ask and answer questions about object locations using prepositions and commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> The focus is on students understanding and responding to commands and questions quickly with gestures and simple words, rather than perfect grammar. Collaborative interaction occurs as students work together to find items and respond. The task is completed by finding all the hidden items.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment is conducted to check learning. Students reflect on their performance and learning process.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes individual students during the "Classroom Treasure Hunt" and the "Show Me" activity, noting their ability to follow commands, respond to "Where is...?" questions, and use target vocabulary and prepositions. A simple "+", "-", or "✓" next to student names for specific criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will verbally respond to a series of 3-4 classroom commands and location questions posed by the teacher, using gestures or one-word answers. Examples: "Point to the purple folder!" "Where is the key?" (Teacher places it behind a box; student says "Behind!") "Show me the small book, please."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key words (e.g., 'where', 'book', colors, prepositions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (nouns, colors, verbs) in response.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of response (correctly pointing, moving, or saying the correct word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in group activities.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "Happy Face" or "Sad Face" card in response to:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I point to the colors: red, blue, yellow?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'in', 'on', 'under'?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Students reflect on their learning journey, and the teacher considers the effectiveness of the lesson and plans for future adjustments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today?" (e.g., "Under!" "Blue!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What game was fun?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The TPR activities and the "Classroom Treasure Hunt" were highly engaging for Kinder students, promoting active speaking and listening. The constant modeling of "Where is...?" and the prepositions helped solidify understanding. Some students still confused 'in' and 'on', and need more repetitive practice. The polite requests ('Show me..., please') were challenging for some, requiring more explicit modeling and choral repetition next time. I should incorporate more specific phonemic awareness activities for K, L, M, N, O in the next warm-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson established the ability to respond to commands and location questions. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students draw and label simple sentences about object locations, such as "The book is on the desk" or "The pencil is in the box," thus transitioning from oral response to written expression of the same concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>